---
/**
 * foodcraft / TrustBN — "Panday Coffee Works"'s four-up fact strip.
 *
 * ROLE. The ruled strip the design sets immediately under the hero, between it
 * and ABOUT. It is FULL-BLEED: four equal tracks running edge to edge, each
 * cell padded 26px 24px, each closed on the right by a grey hairline, with the
 * spine's full-strength ink rule beneath the whole strip. Every cell is one
 * 34px rust Archivo 800 figure over a 10px tracked-caps mono label. It is the
 * band most likely to be read as fact, so not one word in it is written here.
 *
 * NO RAIL, NO INDEX LABEL — and this is the decision a reader is most likely
 * to take for an oversight, so: the design draws TRUST with `border-bottom`
 * ALONE, no `grid-template-columns: minmax(0,52px) minmax(0,1fr)` and no
 * rotated label, and its index sequence runs HERO "00 / Index" straight into
 * ABOUT "01 / The works". TRUST is the one section the sequence skips. Giving
 * this strip an index would therefore not just add a rail the design does not
 * draw, it would push every later section's number one out of step with the
 * design and with the sibling components that read their number off it. Hence
 * `.sec-full` (bottom rule only), which is exactly what the wrapper provides
 * for the full-bleed cases.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (both declared by components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts
 * under the `trust.cells` list spec — add / remove / reorder in the sidebar,
 * maxItems 4):
 *   trust.cells.${i}.num    — the rust figure. set:html, so an entity-bearing
 *                             value ("&lt; 1 hr", "R&amp;D", "&#8369;180")
 *                             prints as the character rather than the source.
 *   trust.cells.${i}.label  — the tracked-caps label under it. ESCAPED, not
 *                             set:html: it is a 10px uppercase phrase where an
 *                             <em> has nothing left to say, and escaping is the
 *                             safer default for the field that is not a figure.
 * There is no third hook. A data-field on any path that schema does not declare
 * is a dead hook no sidebar input owns.
 *
 * DERIVED (computed here, never authored here):
 *   · `rows` — only the cells carrying real text, each still carrying its
 *     ORIGINAL array index `i`, so dropping a blank cell 2 cannot redirect an
 *     edit of cell 3 into slot 2 (R6). The reveal stagger uses the DISPLAY
 *     index instead, because a transition delay is decoration.
 *   · `--pcw-cols` — the track count follows the DATA, capped at the design's
 *     four. This matters because the strip is full-bleed: the hairlines are
 *     drawn per CELL, so a hardcoded repeat(4) would not paint empty ruled
 *     boxes (an empty track paints nothing), but the section's ink rule spans
 *     the viewport regardless — two facts would sit squeezed into the left
 *     quarter of a full-width rule with three quarters of it blank, reading as
 *     a table whose last rows went missing. Following the data keeps the rules
 *     framing content.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, and why:
 *   · NO eyebrow, NO headline, NO lede, NO button, NO photo, NO id. The
 *     design's TRUST has none of them and the schema declares none for `trust`.
 *     In particular there is no .kick here — the family's rust mono eyebrow is
 *     simply absent from this strip, as drawn.
 *   · NO placeholder figure, no zero, no em dash. "4 / Philippine farms" in the
 *     design demo is the DESIGNER filling their own template; a farm count is a
 *     claim only this roastery may make. lib/derive-content-defaults.ts carries
 *     no `trust` key at all, so this strip exists only where a human typed.
 *   · NO section when nothing was typed (R5). The gate counts BODY content —
 *     cells with text — and there is no tag or headline here it could wrongly
 *     count instead. lib/astro-builder.ts already forces visibility.trustBlock
 *     false on a zero-LENGTH array; this gate is the belt to that brace and
 *     catches what a length check cannot see: rows that are PRESENT but blank,
 *     since the sidebar's Add button inserts { num: '', label: '' }. A blank
 *     row surviving would print an empty ruled cell.
 *   · NO onPage() anchor helper, deliberately rather than by omission: this
 *     strip emits no <a> at all, so PAGE_ANCHORS / ANCHOR_ALIAS (menu, about,
 *     gallery, visit, faq, main) would be unreachable dead code in this one
 *     file — which is what a reviewer would flag instead. The remap belongs in
 *     every BN section that does emit an href. TrustBM and TrustBL made the
 *     same call for the same reason.
 *   · NO role="img", NO .plate, NO .cap — there is no image slot in this strip,
 *     so there is nothing to give an accessible name to (R9). Nothing is
 *     aria-hidden either: both the figure and the label are real owner content
 *     and are announced normally, and there is no decorative numeral or icon
 *     here to suppress. No attribute on any element below is written twice.
 *   · NO star row and NO rating. This strip carries no review, so R2's rating
 *     rule has nothing to bind to. If an owner types "4.9" into `num` it is
 *     printed as the TEXT they typed — never re-read as a score, never redrawn
 *     as five glyphs.
 *
 * DECISIONS a reader would otherwise think were oversights:
 *
 *   · The RIGHT-HAND hairline on the LAST cell is the design's. Its sc-for puts
 *     border-right on every repeated cell, so at full bleed the strip closes
 *     with a short vertical rule hard against the right edge of the viewport
 *     while the first cell has none on its left. That asymmetry is drawn, and
 *     it is reproduced rather than "tidied" into a border-left-except-first,
 *     which is a different drawing. Nothing clips it: --r is 0 and this strip
 *     has no overflow to hide.
 *
 *   · The figure does NOT use the wrapper's .fig primitive, and that is the
 *     point. .fig is the MONO figure voice, and it is exactly five
 *     declarations: var(--mono), weight 500, var(--accent), tabular lining
 *     numerals, line-height 1. The design's trust number is the DISPLAY voice:
 *     Archivo 800, 34px, -.03em, line-height 1, in rust. Two of those five
 *     would have survived — the colour and the line-height. The other THREE
 *     (family, weight, the tabular numerals) would each have to be overridden,
 *     and size and tracking added on top, since .fig declares neither. A
 *     section that overrides three of a primitive's five declarations and adds
 *     two more is not reusing it. It is written locally, from tokens only.
 *
 *   · font-family on the figure is var(--disp), not left to inherit var(--sans)
 *     from body. In the design both are Archivo and it inherits; here they are
 *     separate tokens a font pairing could one day split, and an 800-weight
 *     figure at -.03em belongs with the headlines, not with the body copy.
 *
 *   · font-variant-numeric is NOT set. .fig would have brought tabular lining
 *     figures with it; the design sets none, and these cells are four
 *     independent numbers, not a column to align. The design is the spec.
 *
 *   · The label DOES reuse .mono, which already gives var(--mono), 10px,
 *     uppercase and var(--muted) — the design's #5A5C60 exactly, as the native
 *     token value (PageBN notes that the engine remaps --muted to
 *     palette.inkSoft, so it is the design's colour, not a guarantee). FOUR
 *     declarations are local, and only four: letter-spacing .14em (the
 *     design's, against the primitive's .2em — this label is tracked tighter
 *     than the rail and the kicker on purpose), margin-top 9px (the design's
 *     gap under the figure), line-height, and the overflow-wrap noted below.
 *     Only the FIRST overrides the primitive; .mono declares none of the other
 *     three.
 *
 *   · That line-height is PINNED at 1.3. The design sets none on its caps
 *     labels, so in the design document they compute to `normal` — the FONT's
 *     own leading, which is nothing like the page's. PageBN's
 *     `body { line-height: 1.62 }` would otherwise leak in, inflate this 10px
 *     line box and print the design's 9px gap visibly larger, with the cell
 *     taller than drawn. 1.3 is a tight leading that keeps the cell close to
 *     drawn and still lets a two-line label breathe. No number is quoted for
 *     `normal` here: it is a font metric, not arithmetic this file can do, and
 *     an earlier draft of this block asserted one.
 *
 *   · The label's WEIGHT is the primitive's 500, not the design's own. Worth
 *     stating plainly: the design declares no font-weight on this label, so in
 *     the design document it renders at 400 — but the design declares none on
 *     ~40 of its 41 mono elements either, and PageBN answers that by pinning
 *     the family's whole mono voice at 500 across .kick, .mono, .fig, .btn and
 *     .linkc. Restating 400 here would make this the only lighter mono on the
 *     sheet, which reads as a mistake rather than as fidelity. The wrapper is
 *     the contract; this one value follows it. Flagged for review.
 *
 *   · An owner <em> inside `num` is de-italicised and recoloured to --ink
 *     rather than left alone. The wrapper is explicit that this template's
 *     second voice is a COLOUR CHANGE, not an italic, because Archivo has no
 *     italic worth using — left alone the browser would synthesise an oblique.
 *     The figure is already rust, so the counter-voice available to it is ink;
 *     the alternative, killing the italic and giving nothing back, would
 *     silently swallow the owner's emphasis. Reached through :global() because
 *     set:html output sits outside scoped CSS (R3). Flagged for review.
 *
 *   · The read tolerates both content shapes — the schema's { cells: [...] }
 *     and the bare array a few older records still store at content.trust —
 *     while always WRITING the data-field as trust.cells.${i}.*, since that is
 *     the only path the sidebar owns. Note the builder's own visibility check
 *     recognises the { cells } shape alone, so a legacy record is already
 *     hidden before this file runs; the tolerant read is defensive, and costs
 *     one line to make this component correct wherever it is mounted.
 *
 *   · Both values carry overflow-wrap: anywhere. An unspaced string in a 34px
 *     display face is the one thing in this strip that could push the page
 *     sideways at 360 (R8). Nothing here uses a clamp at all — every size is
 *     flat across all three widths — so no floor in the 900 query can outgrow
 *     a cap in the 520 query.
 *
 * Class names are prefixed pcw- for THIS template. They are not a sibling's
 * names re-used: an audit had to rename a whole set that had been copied
 * verbatim between two files in this family.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const trust = content?.trust || {};
// Both shapes are read; only the schema's path is ever written.
const cells: any[] = Array.isArray(trust.cells)
  ? trust.cells
  : (Array.isArray(trust) ? trust : []);

const asText = (v: any) => (v === null || v === undefined ? '' : String(v));

// The ORIGINAL index rides along in `i` (R6). A half-filled row prints the one
// line it has; a wholly empty row prints no cell, so no hairline divides a
// column that is not there (R2).
const rows = cells
  .map((c: any, i: number) => ({ num: asText(c?.num), label: asText(c?.label), i }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.num.trim() || r.label.trim()));

// Follow the data, never past the design's four tracks (the schema caps the
// list at 4 anyway; anything older wraps and picks up a rule above it).
const cols = String(Math.min(Math.max(rows.length, 1), 4));
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="sec-full pcw-strip">
    <div class="pcw-strip-grid" style={`--pcw-cols:${cols}`}>
      {rows.map((r, k) => (
        <div class="pcw-strip-cell reveal" data-delay={String(k % 4)}>
          {r.num.trim() && (
            <div class="pcw-strip-num" data-field={`trust.cells.${r.i}.num`} set:html={r.num}></div>
          )}
          {r.label.trim() && (
            <div class="mono pcw-strip-lab" data-field={`trust.cells.${r.i}.label`}>{r.label}</div>
          )}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* Colour, type and metrics come only from PageBN's token vocabulary
     (--line --accent --ink --muted --disp --mono, via .sec-full and .mono).
     There is not one literal hex in this file (R1).

     Never write a literal end-style-tag inside a comment in here — the parser
     would end the stylesheet on the spot and silently truncate the file. */

  /* The section itself adds nothing: .sec-full already draws the spine's
     full-strength ink rule beneath, and the design gives TRUST no padding of
     its own — the cells do all of it, edge to edge. */
  .pcw-strip { display: block; }

  /* Four equal tracks in the design; here, as many as were typed, up to four. */
  .pcw-strip-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--pcw-cols, 1), minmax(0, 1fr));
  }

  /* 26px 24px with the design's grey hairline on the right of EVERY cell,
     including the last — see the frontmatter note on that asymmetry. */
  .pcw-strip-cell {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 26px 24px;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  /* Only reachable on a record predating the schema's maxItems 4 cap. */
  .pcw-strip-cell:nth-child(n + 5) { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }

  /* The figure: the design's display voice, written out rather than borrowed
     from .fig, which is the mono one. Flat 34px at every width. */
  .pcw-strip-num {
    font-family: var(--disp);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 34px;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -.03em;
    color: var(--accent);
    /* A pasted URL or an unspaced string must not push the page sideways. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* An owner <em> inside the figure: no synthesised oblique, a colour change,
     which is this template's declared second voice. The figure is rust, so the
     counter-voice is ink. :global() because set:html output cannot be reached
     by scoped rules (R3). */
  .pcw-strip-num :global(em),
  .pcw-strip-num :global(i) { font-style: normal; color: var(--ink); }

  /* .mono already carries var(--mono), 10px, uppercase and var(--muted) — the
     design's label treatment, colour included. Four declarations are local, and
     only one of them overrides the primitive:
       · letter-spacing .14em — the design's, tighter than the primitive's .2em
         (the one override; .mono declares none of the three below)
       · margin-top 9px — the design's gap under the figure
       · line-height 1.3 — the design's caps labels set no leading and compute
         to `normal`, the font's own; PageBN's body 1.62 would otherwise leak
         in, inflate this 10px line box and print that 9px gap visibly larger
       · overflow-wrap: anywhere — R8, see the figure's note above */
  .pcw-strip-grid .pcw-strip-lab {
    letter-spacing: .14em;
    margin-top: 9px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── This section's OWN responsive pass (R8). PageBN owns the shared 760 and
        520 type pass; the grid below is ours. Every size in this file is flat —
        no clamps at all — so nothing can grow as the viewport narrows. ────── */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    /* Two up. The interior vertical rule is now the odd cell's right border,
       and every cell past the first pair gains a horizontal one. That rule is
       restated rather than left to lapse: compiled it reads
       .pcw-strip-cell[data-astro-cid-…]:nth-child(n+3) — (0,3,0), which beats
       the base cell's (0,2,0) whatever the source order, but a reader should
       not have to work that out. */
    .pcw-strip-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .pcw-strip-cell:nth-child(n + 3) { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    /* One column. A 10px label at .14em tracking cannot hold the half column
       this breakpoint inherits without splitting mid-word — and because the
       strip is FULL-BLEED that column is 520/2 - 48px of padding = 212px of
       text, not the ~180px a sibling template quotes for the same rule (that
       strip sits inside a 92vw wrap; this one does not).
       The trailing right hairline goes too: at full width it would draw a
       stray vertical line down the edge of the page instead of dividing
       anything, and the horizontal rule between cells takes over the dividing.

       Only the VERTICAL padding moves off the design's 26px 24px, and only
       because stacking spends it four times over. The 24px HORIZONTAL padding
       is the design's and stays — the strip is full-bleed, so at 360px the cell
       is still ~312px wide and nothing is pressing on it.

       The figure stays at the design's flat 34px. It is NOT shrunk: one-up at
       520 the cell is WIDER than two-up at 900, where the figure is left alone,
       so a 520-only reduction would be both a deviation and backwards. Long
       unbroken strings are already handled by overflow-wrap above. */
    .pcw-strip-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .pcw-strip-cell {
      padding: 22px 24px;
      border-right: 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }
    .pcw-strip-cell:first-child { border-top: 0; }
  }
</style>
